  nonce: '4560'  # 4560
  data:  # 4560
    kind: invariant  # 4560
    title: "META: Extension/UFCS call resolution belongs in a shared receiver-type-index linker, not in language analyzers"  # 4560
    status: violated  # 4560
    priority: 2  # 4560
    parent:  # 4560
    tags:  # 4560
    - cross_language_parity  # 4560
    - analysis_quality  # 4560
    - structural  # 4560
    isbefore: []  # 4560
    duplicate_of: []  # 4560
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 4560
    pr_ref:  # 4560
    description: "STATEMENT: Resolution of `x.foo()` to a target OUTSIDE the receiver's type hierarchy — extension methods (Kotlin/C#/Scala/Swift/F#/Dart/Obj-C/Haxe/Pascal), UFCS free functions (D/Nim), library-for-type (Solidity) — is ONE search: a receiver-type-keyed candidate index queried by the call's `receiver_type_hint`, matched by callee short-name. It MUST live in a shared Infrastructure linker, with analyzers only emitting `make_unresolved_edge(...)` + the receiver-type hint — the exact analyzer/linker contract INV-nilud established for inheritance-aware resolution. Currently VIOLATED.\n\nROOT CAUSE: Non-hierarchy call resolution was added per-analyzer as each language's need surfaced, before the shared pattern was recognized. Three ad-hoc implementations of one concept now exist:\n  1. Kotlin — extension-function resolution IN-ANALYZER (WI-visaz/WI-fuhav; kotlin.py:1259-1288 builds `extension_index` keyed by `meta[\"extension_receiver\"]`, emits resolved `ast_call_extension` edges). The exact anti-pattern this invariant names.\n  2. D — UFCS resolution IN-ANALYZER (d_lang.py, `_get_ufcs_template_name` @518 + UFCS branch @~732).\n  3. Rust — trait-impl dispatch via a linker (linkers/rust_trait_dispatch.py; consumes `implements` edges, emits `dispatches_to`).\nPlus ~11 extension-capable languages with NO call-site resolution (C# has only an ambiguity guard, WI-gafat; Scala implicits / Swift `extension` / Dart `extension` / Obj-C categories / F# / Haxe static-extensions parse the members but never resolve calls to them).\n\nWHY ONE SEARCH: extension-method and UFCS resolution build the SAME structure — `receiver_type_name -> [(callable_id, Symbol)]` — from declarations that name a receiver/first-param type, then look up by the receiver's inferred type. Only the analyzer-side DETECTION (what populates the index) and the emitted `evidence_type` differ. `edge_type` stays `calls` (ADR-0023 forbids an `extension`/`ufcs` edge_type — endpoint-derivable). `ast_call_extension` already exists (evidence_types.py:132, conf 0.8); UFCS needs ONE cheap same-axis registry value (`ast_call_ufcs`) — a registry addition, not a new axis (ADR-0028/ADR-0024).\n\nDISTINCT FROM the hierarchy-walk family: inherited-method resolution (inherited_calls.py, `_MRO_WALKERS`) walks the receiver's ANCESTOR chain — a genuinely different search that does NOT belong in this resolver. The MRO walkers stay their own family (see the Scala/Swift `_walk_linearization`/`_walk_left_to_right` items).\n\nPRECEDENT + SPIRIT: This is the sibling of INV-nilud / ADR-0029, which consolidated inheritance-aware resolution out of the Java+Ruby analyzers into one linker (\"the other 12 analyzers shared the same silent gap\"). Same DRY smell (3+ ad-hoc impls of one idea), same fix shape.\n\nBREADTH (grounds the DRY payoff): ~15 supported languages (strong) have a non-hierarchy `x.foo()` mechanism — 13 extension-style + 2 UFCS (D, Nim). The shared linker is built once; each additional language is a small analyzer hint-emitter (mirrors inherited_calls exactly: one linker + per-analyzer hint emission).\n\nPROVENANCE: 3-lens investigation 2026-07-18 (linker-architecture / cross-language-matrix / ADR-spec-spirit). The \"no meaningful shared-code savings\" supposition was found overstated: the shared scaffold (receiver-type-hint front-half + `Edge.create(edge_type=calls...)` emission tail, currently duplicated ~5x in inherited_calls.py) IS worth extracting once the reuse breadth (15 langs) is counted. Full synthesis in the session transcript; see also correctness_strategy_07092026.md.\n\nSATISFACTION CRITERIA: extension/UFCS call resolution routes through the shared receiver-type-index linker for at least the currently-implementing languages (Kotlin migrated off the analyzer; D routed through it), analyzers emit unresolved+hint only, and a property test asserts no analyzer resolves an extension/UFCS call itself (the INV-nilud analogue). Members: substrate WI (below), Kotlin migration WI, D/UFCS (WI-situj re-scoped). C#/Scala/Swift/F#/Dart/Obj-C/Haxe roll-out filed when the program is undertaken."  # 4560
    fields:  # 4560
      statement: "Extension-method and UFCS call resolution (receiver-type-keyed candidate search) MUST live in a shared Infrastructure linker, not duplicated per-analyzer — the INV-nilud contract extended to the non-hierarchy resolution family."  # 4560
      root_cause: "Non-hierarchy call resolution was added per-analyzer as each language surfaced its need, yielding three ad-hoc impls of one search (Kotlin in-analyzer, D in-analyzer, Rust linker) plus ~11 unhandled extension languages; the receiver-type-index search is identical across extension methods and UFCS — only analyzer-side detection and the emitted evidence_type differ."  # 4560
